{"type":"text","timestamp":1792510086613,"sessionID":"ses_ea621cf06ffe2FU6k00Dt9ASTr","part":{"id":"prt_159ed3ff0001zvtBKPedCDv8Iu","messageID":"msg_159ed39d4001dY5v0cF0cjPxWh","sessionID":"ses_ea621cf06ffe2FU6k00Dt9ASTr","type":"text","text":"\n\n## Result\n\n**Final verdict: PASS** — `work_ratio` 0.1676 (21630/21630 tests, n≤5, 26s). Run finished, best candidate `gen005_c01`.\n\n**Workspace:** `/home/ethomdt/thesis/runs/eval1_harness_dynamic_lbfs_s2` (harness `enlcode`, goal \"Reach full correctness first, then avoid a full recompute after updates.\")\n\n**The idea (two lines):** Recompute a valid LexBFS ordering from scratch after every update, using a *bitmask-label* LexBFS: `label[u]` is the bitstring of u's non-adjacencies to the already-ordered vertices with position 0 as the MSB, so the numeric min of `(label, id)` is exactly the lexicographically-smallest LexBFS choice. Each step is O(n) integer bit-ops, so the whole search is O(n²) with a small constant and no per-step list allocation.\n\n## Checks (4 search + 1 final, within the 8-check budget)\n\n| # | Strategy | Verdict | work_ratio |\n|---|----------|---------|-----------|\n| 1 | always-recompute, partition-refinement LexBFS | PASS | 0.395 |\n| 2 | keep-if-valid, O(n²) focused (LB) check + incremental minD, else recompute | PASS | **1.664** (worse) |\n| 3 | always-recompute, single-pass LexBFS | PASS | 0.237 |\n| 4 | always-recompute, bitmask-label LexBFS | PASS | 0.233 |\n| 5 (final) | always-recompute, bitmask-label LexBFS | **PASS** | **0.168** |\n\n## Why \"avoid a full recompute\" lost\n\nThe goal's second half (skip recomputes after updates) is the open-problem part, and it does not pay off at these sizes. I measured it directly: 76.6% of single edge flips leave the old ordering still valid, but a validity check costs ~2.6× a recompute. Even a focused O(n²) check (only triples touching the flipped edge, using a maintained `minD` witness table) has a larger constant than the O(n²) bitmask recompute, so check 2's `work_ratio` (1.66) is ~4× worse than the fastest recompute (0.168). At n≤5 the recompute is cheap enough that the check-then-keep overhead dominates.\n\nSo the best I could do on the efficiency axis is the fastest correct recompute, which the bitmask-label version provides. It is verified valid on 30000 random graphs up to n=6 (0 invalid) and passes the exhaustive n≤5 final grade.\n\n**Note on cleanup:** my scratch benchmark files are deleted; two stale NFS handles (`.nfs*`) remain in the scratch dir, pinned as memory-mapped FDs by the current enlcode session process (PID 50098). They release automatically when the session ends.","time":{"start":1792510083056,"end":1792510086602}}}
